--TEST--
Only the request's own task may `Core\Task::afterResponse`: a `Core\Task::all` child and deferred work itself are refused in the same words — ADR 0072 § 6
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6's last bullet. The queue is the *request's*, so a child task has none,
// and a registration made on one would be dropped where the child ended rather
// than run — which is the failure a refusal at the call site exists to replace,
// the request still being there to decide what to do about it.
//
// The two children below reach that refusal from opposite directions, one from
// inside a group and one from inside deferred work, and what the case asserts
// is that they *agree*: a member that grew a second answer for one of them
// would still look right on that one's own line.
var $fromGroup = Core\Task::all({
    refusal: fn (): string => {
        try {
            Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
                echo "never\n";
            });
            return "accepted";
        } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    },
});

Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
    try {
        Core\Task::afterResponse(fn (): void => {
            echo "never either\n";
        });
        echo "deferred work was allowed to defer more\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        if ($e->message == $fromGroup->refusal) {
            echo "both children refused, in the same words\n";
        } else {
            echo "the two refusals disagree\n";
        }
    }
});

echo "the response\n";
echo "from a group child: ", $fromGroup->refusal, "\n";
--EXPECT--
the response
from a group child: Core\Task::afterResponse: only the request's own task may defer work, and this is a child task — a `Core\Task` child, an isolate, or deferred work itself; hand it back to the request that started you
both children refused, in the same words
